{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"An ETF’s expense ratio isn’t usually charged as a separate fee to your account. The fund pays its operating expenses from its assets, so those costs gradually reduce the ETF’s net asset value and returns.\n\nFor example, a 0.20% annual expense ratio on a $10,000 investment is about $20 per year, reflected over time—not typically billed as a $20 transaction. Your actual cost varies with your investment’s value and holding period.\n\nTrading commissions, bid–ask spreads, and taxes are separate costs and aren’t included in the expense ratio."}}
